    "## Homework 1 - Phase I & II Analysis Using Shewhart $\\bar{X}$ and R/s Charts\n",
    "### Dr. Austin Brown\n",
    "### Kennesaw State University\n",
    "\n", Takaylah Bonds 
    "**DUE: September 19, 2026 via D2L**\n",
    "\n",
    "## Instructions: \n",
    "\n",
    "A company called Blue Ridge Filament Co (BRFC) manufactures 1.75 mm PLA filament for 3D printers, selling to both hobbyist and small-manufacturing customers. Over the last quarter, customer support tickets describing clogging, stringing, and inconsistent print layers have risen sharply, and BRFC recently lost a wholesale account to a competitor over “inconsistent quality.” The plant manager has asked your quality control team to investigate Extruder Line 3, the line most frequently named in the complaint data, and to determine whether the line is running in a state of statistical control. This line is about six months away from needing its heating element replaced, but it could be replaced sooner if necessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "The plant manager is particularly interested in the filament diameter produced (mm), measured inline by a laser micrometer as each spool is produced. Diameter that runs too thin causes under-extrusion and stringing; diameter that runs too thick causes jams and clogs in customers' print heads. Filament diameter is treated as a continuous, normally-distributed quality characteristic for this assignment.\n",
    "\n",
    "Your team pulls a baseline (Phase I) sample of production data from Line 3: 5 spools sampled at random each week, for 25 consecutive weeks. After validating statistical control and process capability, the team can establish an ongoing monitoring plan for Phase II analysis. \n",
    "\n",
    "## Key Specifications\n",
    "\n",
    "| Quantity | Value |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Target diameter | 1.750 mm |\n",
    "| Lower Specification Limit (LSL) | 1.700 mm |\n",
    "| Upper Specification Limit (USL) | 1.800 mm |\n",
    "| Subgroup size (n) | 5 spools per week |\n",
    "| Phase I subgroups (m) | 25 weeks |\n",
    "| Phase II subgroups | 20 weeks |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d8e7c6ae",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Define & Measure\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q1: Briefly describe the purpose of this analysis\n",
    "\n",The purpose of this analysis is to determine whether Extruder Line 3 is producing filament diameter consistently and whether the process is capable of meeting customer specifications.
    "### Q2: Identify the quality characteristic your team will monitor, classify its units and measurement scale.\n",
    "\n",Quality characteristic: Filament diameter
Units: Millimeters (mm)
Measurement scale: Ratio scale
Diameter is a continuous measurement with a true zero value, making it a ratio-scale variable.
    "### Q3: Briefly describe why a Shewhart $\\bar{X}$ and $R/s$ chart would be appropriate in this scenario. What are its limitations?\n",An X̄-R chart and X̄-S chart are appropriate because filament diameter is a continuous measurement and samples are collected in subgroups of 5 spools.These charts monitor: Process average (X̄) Process variation (R or S)
Limitations include them assume the data is approximately normal and may not detect very small process shifts.
    "\n",
    "## Analyze\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q4: Read the Phase I data into R. Calculate and report the estimated process mean and standard deviation using both the $R$ and $s$ methods.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e07e5c78",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Q4 Code ##"
   ]
  }, library(readxl)

phase1 <- read_excel("FilamentDiameter_PhaseI.xlsx")

data <- phase1[, 2:6]

xbar <- apply(data, 1, mean)
R <- apply(data, 1, function(x) max(x) - min(x))
S <- apply(data, 1, sd)

grand_mean <- mean(xbar)
Rbar <- mean(R)
Sbar <- mean(S)

d2 <- 2.326
c4 <- 0.94

sigma_R <- Rbar / d2
sigma_S <- Sbar / c4

grand_mean
sigma_R
sigma_S
